  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nStrong closed cyclone was located over the Missouri valley at \nmidday, with midlevel clouds on the western periphery of the \ncyclone's circulation reaching the eastern zones. Rising surface\npressures are expected for the next several hours into tonight,\nas the associated trailing surface high backdoors southwestward\nacross Kansas. After temperatures in the 40s this afternoon, \ntemperatures will rapidly fall through the 30s and into the 20s \nimmediately after sunset. Nearly ideal radiational cooling is \nexpected especially eastern zones through Thanksgiving morning, \nwhere model consensus places a 1032 mb surface high around \nsunrise. Across eastern and northeast zones, many locations will\nstart Thanksgiving in the teens 6 am Thursday. \n\nThanksgiving will be beautiful with a mix of cirrus and\nsunshine, and very light winds. The cool boundary layer is\nforecast to modify several degrees Thursday, but with very poor\nmixing, most locations will once again spend Thanksgiving\nafternoon in the 40s. \n\nNoticeably milder temperatures are expected Black Friday\nafternoon, into the lower 60s, as the cool air mass is finally\nkicked out, replaced by gentle NWly downslope. \n\nStrong shortwave is forecast to be over the Pacific Northwest at\n6 pm Friday. Global models such as 12z GFS and its ensembles are\nmerging toward previous solutions from other models, showing a \ndeeper wave with more amplification as it crosses the plains \nthis weekend. This stated, it will be difficult to get\nsignificant precipitation as far west as SW KS with such a\nprogressive shortwave. Model consensus places the associated\nsurface low near western Oklahoma Saturday evening, allowing the\nintense cold front to sweep through SW KS followed by intense\nnorth winds. 850 mb wind fields from the various models near \n60 mph at 2k feet AGL suggests wind gusts of 50-60 mph are\nprobable, especially along the preferred US 83 corridor, and\nespecially early Saturday evening before the boundary layer\ndecouples. NBM wind grids Saturday evening are not strong\nenough, and will need to be increased as the event approaches. \n\nRain and/or snow will likely clip across the northeast zones\nbriefly Saturday evening, on the backside of the amplifying\ncyclone's deformation zone. Thermal profiles suggest a quick\nchangeover from rain to snow around the Hays vicinity, and\nimpacts along I-70 will need to be considered given the busy\nholiday travel. Again, very progressive nature will limit\nresidency time of any snow, any snow amounts and limit impacts.\nHowever, wind fields will be intense, and any snow that falls\nwill be accompanied by strong north winds. Will need to watch\nthe modelled progression of this wave; if it can close off\nsooner, further west, winter impacts may expand further west\ninto more of SW KS. As of now, it appears to be much too quick\nto generate much across most of SW KS. Those with travel plans \nSaturday night across the Kansas region are urged to follow \ntrusted sources for weather information regarding this upcoming \nweekend storm system. \n\nSunday will be much cooler, but with no tap of arctic air\navailable, only a seaonable reduction to the 40s is forecast for\nSunday. Mild and dry Monday and Tuesday, with afternoon\ntemperatures quickly rebounding through the 50s.",
